On Dec 1, 4:00 pm, wumpus <wump...@comcast.net> wrote:

> And a ring of slow digestion in my original inventory.

I think slow digestion is rapidly becoming my favorite ring.  I
mean, the ring of levitation is extremely useful at certain points
and very clearly the most convenient source of levitation, and
regeneration can save you a lot of time at certain places, and
a couple of the others are nice here and there (conflict, free
action, ...), but the thing about slow digestion is, wearing it
most of the time (whenever you don't specifically need two
other rings or specifically want to get hungry) makes the
whole hunger problem just go away.

Hunger is a constant interruption that stops you from doing
what you want (or even need) to do and makes you go find
monsters to kill and eat _right now_.  It creates an urgency
that forces you into dangerous situations.  Sometimes it
makes you fight when you're injured.  Sometimes it makes
you descend to dangerous levels you aren't ready for.  It can
prevent you from taking the time to price-check things in
shops, make you put off various other kinds of preparation
(e.g., potion management) until _after_ you fight a bunch of
things that you would have preferred to fight after quaffing
the results of alchemy.

Some roles have more trouble with hunger than others, of
course, and it's also a bigger problem in some parts of the
game than in others.

But the ring of slow digestion completely eliminates it.

Well, not _totally_ completely.  If you're trying for atheist
and foodless conducts at the same time, slow digestion by
itself will not be enough: you'll still get hungry eventually.
But if you're willing to kill and eat things -- even very limited
types of things -- occasionally, the ring of slow digestion
curtails hunger enough so that random monster generation
provides a fully adequate supply of food all the time on any
level.  If you need to make a stash run back up past six
levels you've already cleared out, you can.  And if you
need to turn around afterward and do it again, and then a
third time back-to-back (to, say, transport all of your
spellbooks to a certain stash), you can.  And if you then
want to consolidate and BUC-test all your scrolls and take
the ones you don't need to fountains for blanking, you can,
and then you'll still have enough food left to fiddle around
making holy water and writing and blessing scrolls and
enchanting all your gear to max and so on and so forth.
Hunger doesn't force you to go fight before you're ready,
if you have a ring of slow digestion.

Okay, so there's a lot of food laying around the dungeon,
and you can probably still do all that stuff without slow
digestion, if you're willing to spend a lot of effort keeping
track of food and making sure you always have enough.
But it's tedious, and the ring of slow digestion means you
don't have to mess with it.

It's terribly convenient.


